    {
        "content": "<p>In &lt;= world, lvl 7/11, you are given an \\or statement where both directions are trivially true.  Why doesn't the level complete immediately upon solving either one branch or the other?  Instead it requires you to prove both branches.  Am I missing something obvious about the \\or logic?  This behavior seems like it would lead to a contradiction in the next level, where if a is not equal to b, then only one branch can can be true.</p>",
        "id": 606649774,
        "sender_full_name": "Ian Allen",
        "timestamp": 1782457254
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well you don't just have an <code>∨</code> in the goal but also as an assumption. And if you have an <code>∨</code> in the assumptions, you need to handle each of the cases. Otherwise, you could solve something \"assuming <code>x = 0 ∨ x = 1</code>, <code>x = 0</code>\" which is obviously nonsensical.</p>",
        "id": 606704014,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
